#{[17592186511584 "Our simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)=⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}t+3\\operatorname{sin}3t,\\hspace{0.222em}3\\operatorname{cos}t-3\\operatorname{cos}3t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186514388 "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{1/2+1}}{(\\frac{1}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2}}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/2}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186514390 "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}={x}^{3/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2+1}}{(\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2}}{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{5/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{3/2}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186514446 "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply the power rule with $n=4$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4+1}}{4+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 5{x}^{4} \\\\ &= {x}^{4}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186532677 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{x}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}) \\\\  & =2xy-x{e}^{y} \\\\  & =x(2y-{e}^{y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186534999 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{6}{5}(x+2) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{36}{25}(x+2)^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+3 & =\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{6}{5}(y+3) & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{36}{25}{(y+3)}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{36}{25}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{36}{25}{(y+3)}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{36}{25}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{36}{25}{(y+3)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+{(y+3)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x+2)}^{2}+{(y+3)}^{2}=\\frac{25}{36}.$"] [17592186535012 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$"] [17592186536821 "First, notice that the terms of our series $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ are the coefficients of the Maclaurin series for $g(x).$\n\nPreviously, we found the fourth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x):$\n\n$$\nf(x)\\approx {P}_{4}(x)=2-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}+{\\color{blue}\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{4}}\n$$\n\nTo find the ${x}^{5}$ term of the Maclaurin series of $g(x),$ we simply need to integrate the ${x}^{4}$ term of ${P}_{4}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}{t}^{5}\\,{|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}{x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${a}_{5}$ is the coefficient of $\\frac{1}{40}{x}^{5},$ that is, ${a}_{5}=\\frac{1}{40}.$"] [17592186537903 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/79/798665574b8f0e11d991bb0d934efd01bdeb65840c2e86498be4edbc59578439.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=-{x}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{3}\\,dx-{x}^{3}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}(-3\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}-3\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}(-3{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -3{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}-3{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{-3{r}^{3}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186537908 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\sqrt{x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z-x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}+0+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[\\sqrt{x}]_{x=1}^{x=4}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186537916 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xz\\operatorname{cos}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =0+(-2xz\\operatorname{sin}y)+0 \\\\  & =-2xz\\operatorname{sin}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}(-2xz\\operatorname{sin}y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{4}(-2xz\\operatorname{sin}y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-\\operatorname{sin}y)\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{2}^{4}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [\\operatorname{cos}y]_{y=0}^{y=\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=2}^{z=4} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}-{0}^{2})\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}0)\\cdot (\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186537920 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z) \\\\  & =1+(-1)+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $6$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}1\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =1{∭}_{R}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {6}^{3} \\\\  & =288\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186541058 "The slope of the tangent to $\\mathcal{C}$ equals $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$ To calculate this, we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nIn parts 1 and 2, we found\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{t}^{2}-2}{\\left(\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{5}({t}^{2}-2)({t}^{2}+25).\n$$"] [17592186541073 "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}={x}^{7/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{7}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{7/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/5+1}}{(\\frac{7}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{12/5}}{\\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{12}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{12/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{12}\\cdot \\frac{12}{5}{x}^{12/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{7/5}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186541081 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/fa/fa98f3db31860a463d0a551d5297895e61a6c041d852c04de04c44a194510961.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y{x}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{x}+y.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}y+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3{x}^{2}y+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}y+x)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{-1}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[((1)^{3}y+1)-((-1)^{3}y+(-1))]\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2y+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =[{y}^{2}+2y]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =((2)^{2}+2(2))-0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186541115 "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{4}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{4/5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{4/5+1}}{(\\frac{4}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{9/5}}{\\left(\\frac{9}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{9}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{9/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{9}\\cdot \\frac{9}{5}{x}^{9/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{4/5}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186552430 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={y}^{3}=8{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2+x=2+4\\operatorname{cos}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=u,du=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(8{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}t)-(2+4\\operatorname{cos}t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t\\operatorname{cos}t+8\\operatorname{sin}t+16\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t\\operatorname{cos}t+16\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{0}16{u}^{3}+16u\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }+0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186552492 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={y}^{2}=4{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-xy=-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=u,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt=du,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}t)-(-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\operatorname{cos}t-2{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }6\\operatorname{cos}t{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0}6{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186553576 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e1/e1e4386f3cc75afb63823d74daa27b517e8e47d51fa08c5c9e5bf3ca6f3ca88c.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{6}$ and $Q(x,y)=-{x}^{4}{y}^{3}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{6},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-3{y}^{2}{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 3,\\,-2\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}{y}^{6}-3{y}^{2}{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}({y}^{4}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186554629 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/09/09acd3df62b333bdaeb7553599507d4dbad7412ef2770b00e8fc8855b38a774e.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{2y}+4x$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{7}-{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4-2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-2y)[x]_{-1}^{0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-2y)\\cdot 1\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =[4y-{y}^{2}]_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(4(-1)-(-1)^{2}) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186555811 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b1/b1886315cede967bc6fd606cdf7bf9d6ae4f82264ccce22211562aa590a8fe4a.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-y-3=-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +2)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +4r{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}({(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2}+1)\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}({r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+1)\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\pi }}}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}-r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -3}}}^{3}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}r({r}^{2}+1)}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186555841 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/28/286910d89e35603dc6558b80b11d06736d213fbad8c9c91825f42ff87b26e318.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=2,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=4+y=4+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{5},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,2\\le z\\le 4+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{z}^{2}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{5}}}}{\\int }_{2}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}4+r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}r{z}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186555859 "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,4)$ and $(2,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/47/4719dc10bbf4078e6b266a36b50a82e117b3e775395a8a3e44c0dff4ae56f15b.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=-4x+2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =-4(0)+2 \\\\ 4 & =2\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-4x+2$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=x+4.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =(0)+4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓} \\\\ (0) & =(2)+4 \\\\ 0 & =6\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=x+4$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=-2x+4.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =-2(0)+4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓} \\\\ (0) & =-2(2)+4 \\\\ 0 & =0\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2x+4$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that represented the line in the graph was $y=-2x+4,$ corresponding to answer choice III. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [17592186557238 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3z) \\\\  & =y{e}^{x}+0+3 \\\\  & =y{e}^{x}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}(y{e}^{x}+3)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}y{e}^{x}+3}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186557290 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\sqrt{y})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\operatorname{ln}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y}+0 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}(\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186560984 "According to Green's theorem, if $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\hspace{0.222em}Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x+Q\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I give the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{y}-1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{y},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{e}^{y}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{y}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{5}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{6}{5},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{7}{5}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186561027 "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ \n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and   \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\hspace{1em} & {\\mathbf{r}}_{1}^{'}(t)=4{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}+3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\hspace{1em} & {\\mathbf{r}}_{2}^{'}(t)=-\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}y\\,dx+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{3}\\cdot 4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-t)\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{6}\\,\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & =4[\\frac{{t}^{7}}{7}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{2}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186565439 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/2f/2f36451290eda3db5221e1a5fdbc1647bbedafa97cd47e22d5523d457c4cb83e.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=r.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le r\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}z({(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2}-{(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2})\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{r}^{3}z({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}3\\pi /2}}}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}-4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}}{\\int }_{0}^{r}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{r}^{3}z\\operatorname{cos}2\\theta }}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186565440 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/35/350b2dc98a09957c17f1ab629e21021c30fcccd9443068c722fe7565f04dcbfe.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+9={r}^{2}+9.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le {r}^{2}+9\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}+1}}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +1}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+1}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\pi }}}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{r}^{2}+9}}}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186573262 "To compute $\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b},$ we just need to compute all of the $\\mathbf{i}$'s and $\\mathbf{j}$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b} & =(3\\mathbf{i}-5\\mathbf{j})-(-4\\mathbf{i}+7\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}-5\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{i}-7\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{i})+(-5\\mathbf{j}-7\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =7\\mathbf{i}-12\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186573264 "To compute $\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b} & =⟨2,-1⟩-⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-(-1),-1-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186573275 "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\mathbf{b}+(-3\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{c}) & =2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+(-3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+(\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186573294 "To compute $-2\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} & =-2\\cdot ⟨7,4⟩+⟨9,11⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-14,-8⟩+⟨9,11⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-5,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186573298 "To compute $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b},$ we just need to compute all of the $\\mathbf{i}$'s and $\\mathbf{j}$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} & =(5\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j})+(-\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =5\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(5\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{i})+(-3\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =4\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186573312 "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\mathbf{a}-(3\\mathbf{b}-2\\mathbf{c}) & =4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-(3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}-2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-20 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-20 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-28 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186585088 "Since $\\sqrt[6]{x}={x}^{1/6}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{6}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/6+1}}{(\\frac{1}{6}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/6}}{\\left(\\frac{7}{6}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{6{x}^{7/6}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{6{x}^{7/6}}{7}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{6{x}^{7/6}}{7}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{6}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/6})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{6}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{6}{x}^{7/6-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/6}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186587304 "Since $\\sqrt[4]{x}={x}^{1/4}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/4+1}}{(\\frac{1}{4}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/4})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}{x}^{5/4-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/4}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186592302 "First, we rewrite the second term using the definition of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNext, we put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}{\\operatorname{cos}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}{\\operatorname{cos}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}{\\operatorname{cos}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)} & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x+({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x)}{\\operatorname{cos}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\n$$"] [17592186593223 "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x(\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x}+1) & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x}+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}+\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}}+\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}x}\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}x}}+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{cos}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x \\\\  & =({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)+\\operatorname{cos}x \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x=1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x(\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x}+1)=1+\\operatorname{cos}x.\n$$"] [17592186593227 "First, we factor $2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x$ from the first two terms:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}x+2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x-1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}=\\frac{2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x)-1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x)-1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} & =\\frac{2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x-1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x-({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x)}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}-\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cot}}^{2}x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}x+2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x-1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}={\\operatorname{cot}}^{2}x-1\n$$"] [17592186600830 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/27/27a82956e22b5b2e2de2c02d9d93fb7b7ce57a2b581b88458964eda465e8bebd.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(-1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1={r}^{2}+1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le z\\le {r}^{2}+1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}-(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}3\\pi /2}}}{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{r}^{2}+1}}}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}2\\theta }}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186604621 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/00/00bceda1bdf4f515646b3a1e8476f1eadde721aa52dbc9be9c7e866bcab99d3b.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{7}+y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{5}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{5}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{{x}^{5}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =4\\left(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}\\right){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186614572 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=x \\\\ y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{csc}}^{2}t=1+{\\operatorname{cot}}^{2}t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\operatorname{csc}}^{2}t & =1+{\\operatorname{cot}}^{2}t \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}t & =\\sqrt{1+{\\operatorname{cot}}^{2}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used here that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡t>0$ and $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t>0$ in the range $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, substituting $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=x$ and $\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we obtain\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{\\,1+{x}^{2}\\,}.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is \n$y=\\sqrt{\\,1+{x}^{2}\\,}.$"] [17592186616135 "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(2,4),B(4,2)$ and $C(5,7).$ \n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(2,4)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\ (4) & =(2)+2 \\\\ 4 & =4\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(4,2)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\ (2) & =(4)+2 \\\\ 2 & =6\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(5,7)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\ (7) & =(5)+2 \\\\ 7 & =7\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line."] [17592186616175 "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, we can choose $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,4)$ and $(3,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/ff/ffd1eea07781890c1dd539e2e36f796d9b18bb4b05b33a903279b88b329f9082.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=\\frac{3}{4}x+1.$ \nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =\\frac{3}{4}(0)+1 \\\\ 4 & =1\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{3}{4}x+1$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=4-\\frac{4}{3}x.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(3,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =4-\\frac{4}{3}(0) \\\\ 4 & =4\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓} \\\\ (0) & =4-\\frac{4}{3}(3) \\\\ 0 & =0\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=4-\\frac{4}{3}x$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=\\frac{4}{3}x.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $B(3,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0) & =\\frac{4}{3}(3) \\\\ 0 & =4\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{4}{3}x$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that represented the line in the graph was $y=4-\\frac{4}{3}x,$ corresponding to answer choice II. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186616198 "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-2,7)$, $B(4,3)$ and $C(2,-5).$ \n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-2,7)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+1 \\\\ (7) & =-3(-2)+1 \\\\ 7 & =7\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(4,3)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+1 \\\\ (3) & =-3(4)+1 \\\\ 3 & =-11\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(2,-5)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+1 \\\\ (-5) & =-3(2)+1 \\\\ -5 & =-5\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $B$ does not lie on the line."] [17592186617022 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =36{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =6\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(6\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =36{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =36{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +36{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & =36({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) \\\\  & =36(1) \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36.$"] [17592186620824 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 0 & x \\\\ 3 & 2 & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & x \\\\ 2 & x\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & x \\\\ 3 & x\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0-2x)\\mathbf{i}-(2x-3x)\\mathbf{j}+(4-0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2x,x,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{(-2x)^{2}+{x}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}+16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{61} \\\\ \\sqrt{5{x}^{2}+16} & =\\sqrt{61} \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+16 & =61 \\\\ 5{x}^{2} & =45 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\pm 3.$"] [17592186620854 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ x & 3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 6 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(6-6)\\mathbf{i}-(2x-12)\\mathbf{j}+(x-6)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,12-2x,x-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(12-2x)^{2}+(x-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144-48x+4{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}-12x+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-60x+180}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\ \\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-50x+180} & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-60x+180 & =80 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-60x+100 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-12x+20 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-10) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=2,$ $x=10.$"] [17592186622804 "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨-\\sqrt{3},\\hspace{0.167em}-6,\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\sqrt{3},\\hspace{0.167em}5,\\hspace{0.167em}9⟩ \\\\  & =(-\\sqrt{3})(-\\sqrt{3})+(-6)(5)+(0)(9) \\\\  & =3-30+0 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186622816 "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{r}\\cdot (\\mathbf{p}\\times \\mathbf{q})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{r},$ $\\mathbf{p},$ and $\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}\\cdot (\\mathbf{p}\\times \\mathbf{q}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 6 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)(4\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 6)-4(6\\cdot 2-5\\cdot 6)-2(6\\cdot 0-5\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =-8+72+40 \\\\  & =104.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186622827 "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨1,7,5⟩\\cdot ⟨7,-2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot 7+7\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =7-14-15 \\\\  & =-22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}) &=22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}) &=-22.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186622835 "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}) & =⟨2,5,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,6,3⟩ \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-4)+5\\cdot 6+(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-8+30-3 \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) &=-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186660918 "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 0-4\\cdot (-2))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\hspace{0.167em}((-3)\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 5)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\hspace{0.167em}((-3)\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 5)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =8\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+20\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨8,20,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨0,2,7⟩\\cdot ⟨8,20,1⟩ \\\\  & =0\\cdot 8+2\\cdot 20+7\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0+40+7 \\\\  & =47.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186670092 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +\\frac{9}{4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}.$"]}
